---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="The Living Memory of Music"
  description="The Living Memory of Music records the people who made American music, in their own words: who taught whom, what was passed on, and who was in the room."
  nav="solid"
>
  <div class="pagehead grain grain--dark">
    <p class="kicker">The Living Memory of Music</p>
    <h1>The Living Memory of Music</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">The story of American music, told by the people who made it.</p>
  </div>

  <article class="read grain lm-wide">
    <div class="col reveal">
      <p class="lede">The Living Memory of Music is a record of American music told by the people who were there. A record's credits tell you who played on it. We keep what the credits leave out: who taught whom, what was passed on, and who was in the room.</p>

      <figure class="lm-clip">
        <video src="/assets/living-memory/lm-dolly.mp4" width="1120" height="525" poster="/assets/living-memory/lm-dolly.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="The Living Memory turns and flies in to Dolly Parton."></video>
        <figcaption>Every light is a person, a song or a moment. Here it finds Dolly Parton.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="lm-clip">
        <video src="/assets/living-memory/lm-names.mp4" width="1120" height="525" poster="/assets/living-memory/lm-names.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="The whole Living Memory turning slowly, with names on."></video>
        <figcaption>With the names on, you can see who's in it.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="lm-clip">
        <video src="/assets/living-memory/lm-river.mp4" width="1120" height="560" poster="/assets/living-memory/lm-river.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="Scrolling down one family's river of American song."></video>
        <figcaption>Up close, it becomes a river: who passed the music on, and to whom.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <span class="kicker">Why now</span>
      <p>Many of the people who made this music are still here, and most of their stories have never been written down. When they are gone, the stories go with them, unless someone asks.</p>

      <span class="kicker">How we record</span>
      <p>We record people two at a time, talking to each other, because that's where the best stories come out. We call these conversations Pairs. Each one is about half an hour on a video call, from a computer or a phone, and you can talk longer if you want. There's nothing to prepare.</p>
      <p>A note-taker named Maybelle is on every call. Maybelle is an AI, so nothing you say gets lost.</p>

      <span class="kicker">What happens to your story</span>
      <p>Your name stays on what you said. You see your words before anything is shared, and you can fix anything that's wrong. You can say "off the record" at any point, and nothing you say after that is kept until you say "back on the record." You can also take back your part later. Afterward, you get clips of your conversation to share with family and friends, or online.</p>

      <span class="kicker">Who's behind it</span>
      <p>The Living Memory of Music was started by Jonathan Sexton, a writer and musician in Nashville who is at work on a book called The Sound of Something More. If you have a question, write to him at <a href="mailto:js@inthewld.com">js@inthewld.com</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

